of how we relate our freedom
to historical responsibility.

00:10:40.650 --> 00:10:42.060
And, I've talked about Sartre already,

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:42.893
but it's important to note,

00:10:42.893 --> 00:10:45.540
that Sartre actually changed his position

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:47.160
from his early existentialism,

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:48.030
to his later position.

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:51.150
He started to doubt the
type of pure existentialism,

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:53.490
which is kinda similar
to Marshall's vibe here,

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:56.430
in which social and
political history aren't tied

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:58.020
to our free existence.

00:10:58.020 --> 00:10:59.850
I think during the time where
Sartre changed his mind,

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:01.710
he also got really influenced by Marxism,

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:03.570
and the work of people like Frantz Fanon.

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:05.760
Now, in an interview
that took place in 1969,

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:08.070
Sartre was asked to
describe the relationship

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:09.690
between his early and late work,

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:10.560
and during the interview,

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:12.127
Sartre recalls himself writing that,

00:11:12.127 --> 00:11:14.820
"Whatever his circumstances,
and wherever the site,

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:17.580
a man is always free to
be a traitor or not."

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:18.877
And then, Sartre tells the interviewer,

00:11:18.877 --> 00:11:20.640
"When I read this, I said to myself:

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:23.280
'It's incredible that I
actually believed that.'"

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:24.960
I think we kinda see both sides of that

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:26.160
at the hotel bar here.

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:29.070
Marshall feels like he shouldn't
have to bare responsibility

00:11:29.070 --> 00:11:30.660
for something that he didn't do.

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:32.490
His friend Earnest, is basically saying,

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:35.160
no we can't abstract
ourselves from a real history.

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:37.440
We are tied up in this
whether you like it or not.

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:38.273
When he says,

00:11:38.273 --> 00:11:40.230
"Confession isn't absolution,"

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:43.260
which means you can't
just my bad racism away.

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:46.050
He's saying it has real,
historical, and material effects.

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:47.580
And, Earnest's position,

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:49.500
is similar to Sartre's later synthesis

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:52.140
of existentialism with
historical materialism.

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:54.879
Now, historical materialism is the idea

00:11:54.879 --> 00:11:58.860
that history is shaped
by material conditions,

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:01.140
material activities, economic reality.

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:04.890
History isn't just shaped
by ideas, but what happens.

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:06.508
In books like, "Search For A Method,"

00:12:06.508 --> 00:12:08.520
and, "Critique of Dialectical Reason,"

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:12.000
Sartre gets into this and kind
of has his existential cake,

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.760
while eating his historical
materialism, too.

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:15.765
Now, when this guy Earnest,

00:12:15.765 --> 00:12:19.050
ties together historical
facticity, and economic reality,

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:22.155
he's arguing against the
type of racist bad faith

00:12:22.155 --> 00:12:24.510
that we saw in the work of Lewis Gordon.

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:26.910
He's operating in good faith in a sense,

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:30.150
'cause he's acknowledging
that things like slavery

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:32.539
and colonialism have absolutely shaped

00:12:32.539 --> 00:12:35.730
the material and economic
reality of lots of people.

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:37.680
Some for better. Some for much worse.

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:41.340
And, that his subject group
has benefited from this.

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:44.670
Now, there's a quote from
Frantz Fanon that I like here.

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:45.503
Fanon says,

00:12:45.503 --> 00:12:47.640
"Genuine disalienation
will have been achieved

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:48.720
only when things,

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:50.670
in the most materialist sense,

00:12:50.670 --> 00:12:52.020
have resumed their rightful place."

00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:53.790
I think what Fanon's saying there,

00:12:53.790 --> 00:12:56.364
is that ending a racist disalienation

00:12:56.364 --> 00:12:59.370
isn't just about thinking better ideas,

00:12:59.370 --> 00:13:01.860
or agreeing to better as people,

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:04.560
but about materially
transforming conditions.

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:05.670
And Earnest, in this clip,

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:09.570
seems to be saying that
economic justice, reparations,

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:12.660
is part of what it means
to take responsibility.

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:15.090
You know, what he does
after that is a little much.

00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:17.070
We're not going to show that.

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:18.150
You can watch it for yourself.

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:20.160
But, I think that episode really opens up

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:21.090
a lot of interesting things.

00:13:21.090 --> 00:13:22.290
You might have noted, however,

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:24.921
that the episode doesn't
feature any of the characters

00:13:24.921 --> 00:13:26.790
that we think of when we think of Atlanta.

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:28.620
So, I wanna check out another episode

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:30.720
where we're gonna see
all of our friends again.

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:32.797
Okay, so now I wanna check
out the episode called,

00:13:32.797 --> 00:13:33.810
"White Fashion."

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:35.910
I wanna look at both Darius

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:38.220
and Paper Boi's journeys in this episode.

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:40.740
So, let's start by watching
Darius's arc in the episode,

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:43.560
where he experiences the struggle

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:46.620
of trying to share his culinary
culture with a white lady,

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:49.860
who at first, seems genuinely
curious and interested.

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:51.390
So, let's get started there.

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:52.527
- Are you Naija?

00:13:52.527 --> 00:13:54.540
- Yes, I'm Naija.

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:57.137
- Couldn't tell. Your hair is so igbo.

00:13:58.050 --> 00:14:00.600
- So, this seems like a nice moment.

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:01.980
- River state. Ijaw.

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:05.670
- Where Darius is just sharing his culture

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:06.503
with someone else.

00:14:06.503 --> 00:14:08.100
He's connecting with
this restaurant owner.

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:09.240
This is great.

00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:10.884
- Ugh, I love her.

00:14:10.884 --> 00:14:12.601
- Yeah, she's sweet.

00:14:12.601 --> 00:14:15.087
- I need to get her business card.

00:14:15.087 --> 00:14:16.950
There is a lot of growth potential.

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:17.783
- This is where it gets weird.

00:14:17.783 --> 00:14:20.400
Right when she says there's
a lot of growth potential.

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:22.265
It's like that shift
from looking at a culture

00:14:22.265 --> 00:14:23.820
in an appreciative way,

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:25.193
and now seeing growth potential.

00:14:25.193 --> 00:14:26.700
- I mean, I like fish,

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:27.720
but boneless fish is...

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:28.977
- Is your Shazam working?

00:14:28.977 --> 00:14:32.790
- And even that, that she has
to Shazam to know the music.

00:14:32.790 --> 00:14:33.623
Okay.

00:14:33.623 --> 00:14:37.020
It seems like we're
getting more of that type

00:14:37.020 --> 00:14:39.450
of bad faith that Gordon talks about,

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:42.750
in so much as this woman
doesn't really seem

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:44.559
to be considering the
cultural significance

00:14:44.559 --> 00:14:48.060
of Darius being originally from Nigeria.

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:50.190
What it means to be at this restaurant

00:14:50.190 --> 00:14:51.450
experiencing another culture.

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:54.810
Especially a culture that was
under British colonial power

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:55.950
until the 1960's.

00:14:55.950 --> 00:14:59.670
Sartre said, and he said
this about reading Fanon.

00:14:59.670 --> 00:15:00.655
He said,

00:15:00.655 --> 00:15:01.488
"Have the courage to read it,

00:15:01.488 --> 00:15:03.300
primarily because it will
make you feel ashamed,

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:04.950
and shame, as Marx said,

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:06.420
is a revolutionary feeling."

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:07.560
I think in a sense in this scene,

00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:09.750
we don't see a lot of shame in this woman,

00:15:09.750 --> 00:15:12.330
and that lack of shame seems to allow her

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:15.600
to see everything in this
restaurant as a product,

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:16.950
or a possibility.

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:20.100
When Sartre makes this point
in his preface to Fanon's book,

00:15:20.100 --> 00:15:22.050
he seems to be saying that the effects

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:24.660
of western colonialism of slavery,

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:26.220
should make people feel ashamed,

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:28.830
'cause that shame could lead
them to breaking that cycle

00:15:28.830 --> 00:15:31.980
of racist bad faith and changing things.

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:34.620
I wanna now jump to see what happens.

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:35.453
It's not good.

00:15:35.453 --> 00:15:36.660
We'll just watch it really quickly though.

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:39.393
So, here he goes back to the restaurant.

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:41.850
It's closed down.

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:43.053
- Yo! Darius.

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:45.293
Over here.

00:15:45.293 --> 00:15:46.860
- The white lady is there.

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:51.480
She has started a Nigerian food truck.

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:53.610
- So, she relocated?

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:55.110
Where's Mimi?

00:15:55.110 --> 00:15:55.943
- I don't know.

00:15:55.943 --> 00:15:57.630
We actually never exchanged info, so.

00:15:57.630 --> 00:15:58.650
- Ugh.

00:15:58.650 --> 00:15:59.700
- What's in it?

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:03.300
- It's a peach barbecue
reduction and chunks.

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:04.860
I call it the Darius,

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:05.850
'cause you're from Georgia.

00:16:05.850 --> 00:16:07.710
- She even named a dish after him.

00:16:07.710 --> 00:16:08.828
So, like I said before,

00:16:08.828 --> 00:16:10.020
there's some irony here,

00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:14.040
because the country of Nigeria
was colonized by Britain.

00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:16.860
And now, this British woman
has basically colonized

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:18.210
a Nigerian restaurant.

00:16:18.210 --> 00:16:19.506
In the same book I just quoted

00:16:19.506 --> 00:16:21.690
where Sartre's talking about Fanon,

00:16:21.690 --> 00:16:22.523
Fanon himself says,

00:16:22.523 --> 00:16:25.710
"When I look for man in European
lifestyles and technology,

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:27.690
I see a constant denial of man,

00:16:27.690 --> 00:16:29.400
and avalanche of murders."

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:32.370
I think similar to the bad
faith black existentialism,

00:16:32.370 --> 00:16:33.600
Fanon is here pointing out

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:35.760
that when European
lifestyle and technology

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:38.970
denied the humanity of black
people and their culture,

00:16:38.970 --> 00:16:40.890
it allows them to instrumentalize them,

00:16:40.890 --> 00:16:44.310
and use them as they would
any other product or resource.

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:45.143
And in this case,

00:16:45.143 --> 00:16:47.190
this woman doesn't seem to be recognizing

00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:50.280
the humanity of a Nigerian woman,

00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:51.887
and her restaurant and her culture.

00:16:51.887 --> 00:16:54.780
She sees a concept that can be used

00:16:54.780 --> 00:16:57.300
to create profit for herself.

00:16:57.300 --> 00:16:58.133
And, of course,

00:16:58.133 --> 00:17:00.660
Darius seems to be realizing
this all in real time.

00:17:00.660 --> 00:17:02.250
This is from Sartre's preface once again.

00:17:02.250 --> 00:17:03.083
He says,

00:17:03.083 --> 00:17:05.040
"You who are so Liberal, so humane,

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:07.560
who take the love of culture
to the point of affectation,

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:09.600
you pretend to forget
that you have colonies

00:17:09.600 --> 00:17:11.430
where massacres are
committed in your name."

00:17:11.430 --> 00:17:14.370
I think when Sartre here
talks about loving a culture

00:17:14.370 --> 00:17:15.420
to the point of affectation,

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:18.110
he's getting at this idea that
people love cultural products

00:17:18.110 --> 00:17:20.040
or aspects of culture,

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:22.320
while conveniently forgetting,

00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:24.270
once again kind of in that bad faith way,

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:26.490
that massacres have been
committed in the name

00:17:26.490 --> 00:17:27.540
of those peoples countries.

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:28.373
In this case,

00:17:28.373 --> 00:17:30.930
we see that with this
woman who's from a culture

00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:32.124
that colonized Nigeria,

00:17:32.124 --> 00:17:35.340
now micro-colonizing
a Nigerian restaurant.

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:37.230
Now, while all this is
happening in the same episode,

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:40.260
Paper Boi is having a similar
experience on a larger scale.

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:42.240
And, it involves a French fashion company.

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:45.813
Okay, so this is presumably a gift,

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:50.087
to that guys grandkid
from the fashion designer.

00:17:50.087 --> 00:17:52.358
- Whoa!

00:17:52.358 --> 00:17:55.560
- Okay. It's a jersey that
says Central Park Five.

00:17:55.560 --> 00:17:57.660
Many of you know that
the Central Park Five,

00:17:57.660 --> 00:18:01.126
were a group of young
black men in New York,

00:18:01.126 --> 00:18:04.710
who were falsely accused
of murdering a woman.

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:06.570
They were exonerated, but
they spent time in jail.

00:18:06.570 --> 00:18:07.770
It was very, very bad.

00:18:07.770 --> 00:18:10.140
And, now we're using that trauma and pain

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:12.810
to make clothes for
presumably very rich people.

00:18:12.810 --> 00:18:15.270
- We want to apologize to
the community properly.

00:18:15.270 --> 00:18:16.170
- Okay.

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:17.003
- With your help.

00:18:17.003 --> 00:18:18.270
- And now, the same fashion company,

00:18:18.270 --> 00:18:22.470
wants Paper Boi to be one of the faces

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:27.470
of their apologies for doing a racism.

00:18:28.050 --> 00:18:30.480
- You're not worried about, you know,

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:32.373
what the streets think?

00:18:32.373 --> 00:18:33.683
- The streets?

00:18:33.683 --> 00:18:35.373
(laughs) Man, (beep) the streets.

00:18:35.373 --> 00:18:36.660
Man, I've shot (beep).

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:39.480
You tryna tell me that
you wouldn't take any

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:41.040
of these free designer gifts?

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:42.060
- Here we are seeing this tension, right,

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:45.360
where Paper Boi's taking
advantage of the situation

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:47.708
to get some designer clothes,

00:18:47.708 --> 00:18:50.253
and Earn is asking the question,

00:18:51.739 --> 00:18:53.490
can we do something more here.

00:18:53.490 --> 00:18:54.930
- Then I'd start my own program.

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:57.630
Help out black entrepreneurs
and business owners.

00:18:57.630 --> 00:19:00.120
Like a "We invest in your hood" campaign.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:02.473
- Earn here, is encouraging
Paper Boi to take advantage

00:19:02.473 --> 00:19:05.010
of the situation and
do something positive,

00:19:05.010 --> 00:19:06.919
and use his subject position,

00:19:06.919 --> 00:19:09.540
as a internationally famous rapper,

00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:11.940
to get this fashion
company to do something

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:13.710
to empower Urban communities,

00:19:13.710 --> 00:19:16.170
and to even build his own foundation.

00:19:16.170 --> 00:19:18.630
There's a Fanon quote that I
think applies a little bit here

00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:19.463
where Fanon says,

00:19:19.463 --> 00:19:20.520
"For Europe, for ourselves,

00:19:20.520 --> 00:19:21.780
for humanity, comrades,

00:19:21.780 --> 00:19:24.210
we must make a new start,
develop a new way of thinking,

00:19:24.210 --> 00:19:26.100
and endeavor to create a new man."

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:28.230
Fanon thought it was possible
to create a new world,

00:19:28.230 --> 00:19:29.280
and a new humanism.

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:30.630
And by a new humanism,

00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:33.372
he means like a new universal
conception of humanity.

00:19:33.372 --> 00:19:36.477
He thinks we do this by
first rethinking the type

00:19:36.477 --> 00:19:39.420
of western humanism associated with

00:19:39.420 --> 00:19:41.160
the western philosophical tradition,

00:19:41.160 --> 00:19:43.410
and white European humans.

00:19:43.410 --> 00:19:48.090
So, rather than just making the
category human, or humanism,

00:19:48.090 --> 00:19:49.860
this white European human thing.

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:51.510
He wants it to apply to everyone.

00:19:51.510 --> 00:19:52.343
But, of course,

00:19:52.343 --> 00:19:53.890
Earn's ideas here assume a world

00:19:53.890 --> 00:19:55.920
where a multi-national fashion company,

00:19:55.920 --> 00:19:59.160
would have a genuine interest
in helping use their capital

00:19:59.160 --> 00:20:02.332
to directly invest in black communities.

00:20:02.332 --> 00:20:04.830
We see how this goes a little bit,

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:06.450
when we get to the press conference,

00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:10.495
where Paper Boi has to
apologize for racism.

00:20:10.495 --> 00:20:13.113
So, let's cut there really quick.

00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:16.020
- Clermont with Le Parisien

00:20:16.020 --> 00:20:17.386
- Um, Paper Boi,

00:20:17.386 --> 00:20:20.280
after this is racism over?

00:20:20.280 --> 00:20:22.710
- Okay, so she just asked Paper Boi.

00:20:22.710 --> 00:20:25.290
She said, "Paper Boi,
after this is racism over?"

00:20:25.290 --> 00:20:27.990
- Um, (beep) no.

00:20:27.990 --> 00:20:30.310
Obviously. (laughs)

00:20:30.310 --> 00:20:32.820
I mean look at the porn industry, right?

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:37.064
- I think what he means, is
that with our new initiative,

00:20:37.064 --> 00:20:41.823
we believe racism will be done by 2024.

00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:47.310
- He said racism will be done by 2024.

00:20:47.310 --> 00:20:49.680
After that experience and
seeing the press conference,

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:52.328
Paper Boi wants to sort of
listen to Earn's advice.

00:20:52.328 --> 00:20:54.240
He comes up with a pitch.

00:20:54.240 --> 00:20:56.670
- I just got this idea about
black people making money.

00:20:56.670 --> 00:20:57.503
You know what I'm saying?

00:20:57.503 --> 00:20:59.280
And, putting them black
dollars in black hands,

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:01.260
and then we just support
black business, right?

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:02.250
'Cause if you take them dollars,

00:21:02.250 --> 00:21:03.390
and put them back in your neighborhood,

00:21:03.390 --> 00:21:04.560
your neighborhood will grow.

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:05.760
So, I figure we just call it

00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:09.000
the reinvest in your hood campaign, right?

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:09.833
Think about it.

00:21:09.833 --> 00:21:12.060
Reinvest in your hood.

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:14.760
Reinvest in your hood.

00:21:14.760 --> 00:21:16.203
Reinvest in your hood.

00:21:17.777 --> 00:21:21.750
- Again, we see Paper Boi truly wanting

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:22.830
to do something positive.

00:21:22.830 --> 00:21:24.060
Create some type of change.

00:21:24.060 --> 00:21:26.970
Now, here's the ad that the
fashion brand comes up with.

00:21:26.970 --> 00:21:29.601
- [Paper Boi] Reinvest in your hood.

00:21:29.601 --> 00:21:30.434
Think about it.

00:21:30.434 --> 00:21:31.380
'Cause if you take them dollars,

00:21:31.380 --> 00:21:32.550
and put them back in your neighborhood,

00:21:32.550 --> 00:21:34.294
your neighborhood will grow.

00:21:34.294 --> 00:21:37.490
- Oh boy.
- Reinvest in your hood.

00:21:37.490 --> 00:21:40.920
- We got cowboys. We got kids and goats.

00:21:40.920 --> 00:21:42.720
- We're all from some hood.

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:45.040
- We have old white ladies.
- We're all from some hood.

00:21:45.040 --> 00:21:47.490
- Okay, so this ad seems to be precisely

00:21:47.490 --> 00:21:49.890
what someone like Fanon
was worried about, right?

00:21:49.890 --> 00:21:52.800
It takes a sort of white Liberal humanism,

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:54.480
and says that black people count,

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:55.590
but so do rich white ladies,

00:21:55.590 --> 00:21:57.450
because they're all from some hood.

00:21:57.450 --> 00:21:59.970
Sartre grew more cynical in his later work

00:21:59.970 --> 00:22:01.260
in the "Critique of Dialectical Reason."

00:22:01.260 --> 00:22:03.720
Sartre argued that while
human freedom is capable

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:05.820
of rising up and trying
to change the world,

00:22:05.820 --> 00:22:07.323
and he calls these moments apocalypses.

00:22:07.323 --> 00:22:09.300
That these movements
of freedom and equality

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:11.550
run up against institutions.

00:22:11.550 --> 00:22:13.770
So, those could be states,
governments, businesses.

00:22:13.770 --> 00:22:15.660
And, here it seems like
Paper Boi's attempt

00:22:15.660 --> 00:22:18.540
to use his freedom in a certain situation

00:22:18.540 --> 00:22:19.710
to do something positive,

00:22:19.710 --> 00:22:21.685
was met with the power of the institution

00:22:21.685 --> 00:22:25.470
that is a fashion company
and a marketing ecosystem,

00:22:25.470 --> 00:22:27.144
in which inclusion only matters

00:22:27.144 --> 00:22:29.490
when it's including new consumers,

00:22:29.490 --> 00:22:32.220
who could potentially buy their clothes.

00:22:32.220 --> 00:22:33.053
Sartre say,

00:22:33.053 --> 00:22:34.590
"Demanding yet denying the human condition

00:22:34.590 --> 00:22:36.690
makes for an explosive contradiction."

00:22:36.690 --> 00:22:38.910
And, it seems like that's
what's happening here.

00:22:38.910 --> 00:22:42.210
They're both demanding and
denying the human condition

00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:43.770
in this case for black people,

00:22:43.770 --> 00:22:47.220
and people effected by their
very messed up ad campaign.

00:22:47.220 --> 00:22:48.690
So, the last clip I wanna look at.

00:22:48.690 --> 00:22:51.750
You know, we have the
aftermath of Paper Boi

00:22:51.750 --> 00:22:54.054
seeing the commercial that
the fashion company made.

00:22:54.054 --> 00:22:55.800
- (beep), I know what the
(beep) the Red Cross is.

00:22:55.800 --> 00:22:56.700
It's the worst one.

00:22:56.700 --> 00:22:57.900
- I mean this is intense
here in this scene,

00:22:57.900 --> 00:23:00.513
'cause you see Paper Boi, Alfred,

00:23:01.680 --> 00:23:03.780
kind of like losing hope.

00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:04.905
- Relax. Relax.
- Stop playing with me, man.

00:23:04.905 --> 00:23:05.738
- Relax. Relax.

00:23:05.738 --> 00:23:06.571
- What I wanna do is
go in there with a bat

00:23:06.571 --> 00:23:07.404
and knock they asses.

00:23:07.404 --> 00:23:08.485
- You wanna go in there...
- Yeah, I wanna do that.

00:23:08.485 --> 00:23:09.906
- And beat their asses?
- Why you ain't mad?

00:23:09.906 --> 00:23:13.500
- (laughs) Look, ask yourself this.

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:15.000
Why?

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:17.250
Why would a company make a project

00:23:17.250 --> 00:23:20.310
that would teach black people
to stop buying their products,

00:23:20.310 --> 00:23:21.565
and reinvesting their own?

00:23:21.565 --> 00:23:22.590
- (mouth smacks) Man.

00:23:22.590 --> 00:23:24.488
- Why would they fund their own demise?

00:23:24.488 --> 00:23:27.090
- He says, "Why would they
fund their own demise?"

00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:28.080
- Look, I told you.

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:30.960
I've been doing this social
justice thing a long time.

00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:31.860
- Something about that phrasing.

00:23:31.860 --> 00:23:33.870
I've been doing this social justice thing.

00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:36.240
Here this character is
basically telling Paper Boi

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:38.940
that the type of change
he wants is impossible

00:23:38.940 --> 00:23:39.773
in the current system.

00:23:39.773 --> 00:23:42.030
Think back to Sartre's notion
of institutions, right?

00:23:42.030 --> 00:23:42.863
In this case,

00:23:42.863 --> 00:23:45.120
the institution of the fashion
industry and capitalism,

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:47.730
are stopping what Paper Boi wants to do.

00:23:47.730 --> 00:23:49.200
In a sense we see the limits

00:23:49.200 --> 00:23:51.900
of the optimism we find in Fanon's work.

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:55.290
And, this brings up a
concept call Afropessimism,

00:23:55.290 --> 00:23:58.020
which is a philosophy attributed

00:23:58.020 --> 00:23:59.850
to the work of Frank Wilderson.

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:02.640
And, unlike Fanon's
call for a new humanism

00:24:02.640 --> 00:24:04.920
that would move past
colonization and racism,

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:08.310
Wilderson doesn't think
that racism is fixable.

00:24:08.310 --> 00:24:09.187
He says at one point,

00:24:09.187 --> 00:24:12.210
"Blackness is coterminous with slaveness."

00:24:12.210 --> 00:24:13.530
And, in a New Yorker review,

00:24:13.530 --> 00:24:16.207
they actually compare his
position to Fanon's and say,

00:24:16.207 --> 00:24:18.450
"That for Wilderson,
Fanon's cup is too full.

00:24:18.450 --> 00:24:20.850
Other previously colonized
peoples are indeed human,

00:24:20.850 --> 00:24:21.720
but not black people.

00:24:21.720 --> 00:24:24.420
One of the bleakest aspects
of Afropessimist thought,

00:24:24.420 --> 00:24:26.880
is it's denial that there
is a meaningful analogy

00:24:26.880 --> 00:24:28.920
between blacks and other nonwhites."

00:24:28.920 --> 00:24:31.140
And this is all very intense sounding,

00:24:31.140 --> 00:24:32.790
but for someone like Wilderson,

00:24:32.790 --> 00:24:34.110
the issues at play here,

00:24:34.110 --> 00:24:37.470
the legacies of things like
slavery, and colonization,

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:38.670
and anti-black racism,

00:24:38.670 --> 00:24:41.370
are baked to deeply into society,

00:24:41.370 --> 00:24:43.920
and into western
capitalism to just change.

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:46.140
Especially, not via a marketing campaign.

00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:47.910
Now, I think then with that in mind,

00:24:47.910 --> 00:24:50.070
we could read this conversation
that Paper Boi has,

00:24:50.070 --> 00:24:54.240
as this idea that a system
built on capitalism,

00:24:54.240 --> 00:24:55.620
and colonization, and slavery,

00:24:55.620 --> 00:24:58.776
is never gonna have space
for real black liberation

00:24:58.776 --> 00:24:59.790
built into it.

00:24:59.790 --> 00:25:01.590
So, the best thing that can happen,

00:25:01.590 --> 00:25:05.040
is that some rich
companies funnel some money

00:25:05.040 --> 00:25:06.120
into some non-profits,

00:25:06.120 --> 00:25:08.640
so some people can do
some good stuff with that.

00:25:08.640 --> 00:25:09.750
And in this episode,

00:25:09.750 --> 00:25:10.830
it seems like we kind of go from

00:25:10.830 --> 00:25:14.564
a more Fanon inspired optimism, humanism,

00:25:14.564 --> 00:25:18.420
to a more Afropessimistic position,

00:25:18.420 --> 00:25:20.400
Allah the word of Wilderson.

00:25:20.400 --> 00:25:21.330
Now, in this episode,

00:25:21.330 --> 00:25:23.790
both Paper Boi and Darius run up against

00:25:23.790 --> 00:25:24.900
the limits of freedom,

00:25:24.900 --> 00:25:26.370
and their own existential control.

00:25:26.370 --> 00:25:29.190
Darius sees how an earnest,

00:25:29.190 --> 00:25:30.936
and seemingly kind attempt,

00:25:30.936 --> 00:25:32.490
to share a bit of his culture

00:25:32.490 --> 00:25:34.110
with a new friend can backfire,

00:25:34.110 --> 00:25:36.630
when that person just views
that culture as a product

00:25:36.630 --> 00:25:38.490
to be repackaged in a food truck.

00:25:38.490 --> 00:25:40.830
And then, Paper Boi runs
up against the institutions

00:25:40.830 --> 00:25:42.450
of capitalism and consumerism,

00:25:42.450 --> 00:25:44.940
and realizes that no
amount of good intentions

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:46.890
can make those systems less corrupt.

00:25:46.890 --> 00:25:48.840
Okay, so it seems that in this season,

00:25:48.840 --> 00:25:51.630
by introducing a level harsh impossibility

00:25:51.630 --> 00:25:52.740
into the lives of its characters,

00:25:52.740 --> 00:25:54.690
the show is trying to explore a sense

00:25:54.690 --> 00:25:56.070
of finitude in the world.

00:25:56.070 --> 00:25:58.590
The idea that there's
somethings that we can't change

00:25:58.590 --> 00:26:00.330
and maybe that we'll
never be able to change.

00:26:00.330 --> 00:26:02.340
Whether it be matters of racial identity,

00:26:02.340 --> 00:26:04.620
the histories of
colonialism and capitalism,

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:06.390
or even our individual identities.

00:26:06.390 --> 00:26:07.223
And in doing this,

00:26:07.223 --> 00:26:10.980
the show is following a bit
of a post-existentialist

00:26:10.980 --> 00:26:12.630
philosophical agenda, right?

00:26:12.630 --> 00:26:13.463
With Sartre,

00:26:13.463 --> 00:26:16.472
we're seeing how a seemingly
limitless existential freedom

00:26:16.472 --> 00:26:19.230
can run up against the
limits of institutions.

00:26:19.230 --> 00:26:21.990
With Fanon, we're
exploring the alienation,

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:24.900
and unconscious violence
that colonialism and racism

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:26.010
have cooked into society,

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:28.980
and asking whether or not
a new humanism is possible.

00:26:28.980 --> 00:26:31.080
With Wilderson, and the Afropessimist,

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:32.750
the show seems to be
asking if these problems

00:26:32.750 --> 00:26:34.950
can ever be solved,

00:26:34.950 --> 00:26:38.330
and considering that
maybe this type of racism

00:26:38.330 --> 00:26:40.980
is a permanent feature of western culture.

00:26:40.980 --> 00:26:41.813
And, of course,

00:26:41.813 --> 00:26:43.470
it's kinda fitting that
this all takes place against

00:26:43.470 --> 00:26:45.120
the backdrop of Europe.

00:26:45.120 --> 00:26:47.670
A place that in one
sense seems more forward

00:26:47.670 --> 00:26:49.200
and progressive than America,

00:26:49.200 --> 00:26:52.830
but is also directly haunted
by the colonial violence

00:26:52.830 --> 00:26:53.730
of its past.

